# Eywa — Cursor Rules

## Project

Production-grade Go framework for conversational AI agents. Hexagonal architecture.
19 independent Go sub-modules (each has its own `go.mod`).

## Architecture — Hexagonal (Non-Negotiable)

- `internal/implementation/` = domain logic. NEVER import `internal/infrastructure/`.
- `ports/` = interfaces only. No structs, no functions, no init side effects.
- All dependencies injected via constructors — no globals except `dbg.globalLogger`.
- Sub-modules never import each other — only root `eywa` module is shared.
- New capability: define port interface → implement adapter → inject via builder.

## Domain Terms

Always use: `Weave`, `Spirit`, `Pulse`, `Oracle`, `Bond`, `Voice`, `Receptor`,
`Scout` (sequential, fail-open), `Lore` (RAG), `Imprint`, `Vigil`, `Rite`,
`Keeper`, `Conduit` (MCP), `Archivist`, `Action`.

Never invent synonyms ("agent" for Spirit, "handler" for Voice, etc.).

## SOLID

- Single Responsibility: one struct, one job. Split if you need "and".
- Open/Closed: extend via new port implementations, not modifying orchestration core.
- Liskov: all port implementations must honor the full contract.
- Interface Segregation: narrow ports — don't pass god-objects.
- Dependency Inversion: domain depends on `ports.*`, never concretions.

## Clean Code

- Names reveal intent. Booleans: `is`/`has`/`can` prefix.
- Functions do ONE thing. Max ~40 lines before questioning design.
- Early return over nesting.
- No hidden side effects.
- Comments explain WHY only. Never WHAT. No godoc on self-explanatory functions.
- Errors: always return, wrap with `fmt.Errorf("context: %w", err)`.
- `log.Fatal`/`os.Exit` never in library code.

## Testing

- TDD: write failing test first.
- Table-driven tests for variation cases.
- Always `-race` flag.
- No real HTTP calls — use `httptest.NewServer` or injectable stubs.
- `t.Skip` when external services not configured.
- Test inside sub-module dir: `cd redis && go test ./... -race`

## Security — Never Break

- SSRF: `netutil.ValidateURL` before any outbound HTTP.
- Body limits: `io.LimitReader` — never bare `io.ReadAll(resp.Body)`.
- API keys: SHA-256 hash + `crypto/subtle.ConstantTimeCompare`.
- In-memory maps on external input: hard cap required.
- New unauthenticated endpoint: add rate limiting middleware.

## Go Conventions

- Propagate caller `ctx` — no `context.Background()` mid-chain.
- Cleanup goroutines: `context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 10*time.Second)`.
- Sentinel errors exported; error constructors unexported.
- `sync.RWMutex` for read-heavy shared state.

## Never Do

- `log.Fatal` in library code → return error
- `io.ReadAll(resp.Body)` → use LimitReader
- Outbound HTTP without SSRF check
- Import infra from domain → define port, inject adapter
- Sub-modules importing each other
- Speculative abstractions (YAGNI)
- Comments describing what code does
- Skip `-race` in tests
- AI attribution in commits
